Ladybird’s permission manager follows Chrome’s model for how long a
decision lasts. An “Allow this time” grant ends when no tab shows the
site, after 5 minutes with no visible tab, or after 16 hours at most;
Chrome’s OneTimePermissionsTracker uses the same limits
(kOneTimePermissionTimeout and kOneTimePermissionMaximumLifetime in
permission_context_base.h). Three dismissals or four ignored prompts
put a site under a 7-day embargo during which it reads as denied, as in
Chrome’s PermissionDecisionAutoBlocker (kDefaultDismissalsBeforeBlock,
kDefaultIgnoresBeforeBlock, kDefaultEmbargoDays). A private session
inherits the ordinary session’s blocks but not its grants, which is
how Chrome registers geolocation (INHERIT_IF_LESS_PERMISSIVE in
content_settings_registry.cc).

Gecko and WebKit differ, deliberately not followed here. Gecko’s
PermissionUI.sys.mjs keeps an unremembered answer for an hour per tab
(privacy.temporary_permission_expire_time_ms), and only a checked
“Remember this decision” box makes it persistent. WebKit’s
WKWebGeolocationPolicyDeciderIOS.mm applies an answer automatically
only once the same answer has been given twice within a day
(kGeolocationChallengeThreshold).
